# 💻*Command-line Cheatsheet*💻
**commands we will be going over:**
```
1. pwd
2. ls
3. cd <folder>
4. cd ..
5. mkdir <name>
6. touch <name>
```
## Introducing.. `mkdir <name>`!
mkdir <_name_> *creates a new **folder**!
> funfact about it: these → '<__>' aren't necessary! you can simply create a folder by coding "mkdir" **press space** before typing whatever title you wish to give your folder! Here's an example: 'mkdir project' will create a folder named "project".
___
## Introducing.. `touch <file>`!
touch <_file_> *creates a new **file**!
> funfact: similar to mkdir, touch <_file_> does not require '<__>', you can simply code "touch" **press space** before typing the title you wish to give your file! Here's an example: 'touch notes' will create a file named "notes.md"
___
## Introducing.. Both `cd ..` and `cd <folder>`!
- **cd <_folder_>** changes your directory *(in simple terms: it helps you move to another folder)*

- **cd ..** makes you go back a directory *(in simple terms it is: used to go back to the previous folder)*
> Funfact: cd <_folder_> and cd .. are opposites! one takes you to another folder the other takes you to the previous folder! Also, similar to mkdir and touch, the '<__>' are not required for cd <_folder_>!
___
## Introducing...  `pwd!`
pwd shows current directory *(or in simpler terms: It shows the folder you're currently in)
> this is really helpful whenever you're unsure or forgot on which folder you're in.
## Introducing.. `ls`!
ls lists directory files (or in simpler form: It lists out all the files you've created inside your folder)
> this is really helpful when you want to chech how many files you've added to a folder or when you just want to double check you have all the files you need or check that you named them correctly!
